A||||33||TRiAD||So, is this a problem? (greusome pic inside.)||||1||
Z||000000||TRiAD||12-03-2001||04:02 PM||||So, I'm finally tearing into the car to see why the front end has funny gaps in the panels, and why the front turnsignals don't line up with the holes in the front fascia, and I find these...<P> <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0OQAACP0TJDahq7QTV5Xtef4P8TrvCTNLL!*1jwlpUk2nQxKupHjnBXLcCQEkJPMBts7PpgLYVWrLcwgpzGi4yGCiCKPhTCkA/shock-close.jpg"> <P>My shocks in front both look like this...<BR>Is this a problem? (ugh!)<P>AND...<P>Can anyone tell me what's wrong with this fron end, under the fascia?<P> <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0OwAAAPAUx2pfz5kYscXczhv29Kx7MhN54y8XnH0z1CBOaaZcaZckhtrhDy2hhrsnoye!ryc4a6Ctj**VvrOsWuQiBDTONA!*/front-exposed.jpg"> <P><BR>Any and all help is appreciated. Thanks.<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0RAAAAN8VWdTblRDE93zkbJ*AbWuOFSwE0rtNS2h23KCe3aGgj1O!JDqud!IoFt1NPvdq6rh9ef*226xJh!nSf*vv2Id5gL8WoDCkDX*tCeM/1985-Signature-2.gif">    <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B> ~ Photoshop rendition of planned modifications</I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||REDHOTT88||12-03-2001||04:08 PM||||Triad.. Is that the SAME car as in your sig? Or is that just a decoy?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> What the H*** happened?<P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed<BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red & shiny all over!)</FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||TRiAD||12-03-2001||04:19 PM||||This is the actual pic of the car that the sig was made from...<P> <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0OwBjb34TCymh!5RGOwQOQZLgrg4PyD3CdEeJuDSXWegiCObwLuRPPK1wXIPpJ8jMLfzieUn1q8biTvEazSovbWq*oYpqHaJP/fs-church-2.JPG"> <P>There were odd gaps in the front bumper/fascia seam as well as the fascia/fender seams, and the front signals didn't line up with the holes in the fascia, so I am attempting to go through and re-align the hood, and determine if there's anything wrong under the fascia...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroMontreal||12-03-2001||04:39 PM||||Ouch... I don't think thats supposed to look like that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR> -Alex <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigfieromontreal_1.gif">   <BR>1986 Fiero GT, STOCK Wingless, <BR>President of FieroMontreal... <A HREF="http://www.fieromontreal.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieromontreal.com</A> <BR>and Regional Director of CFOG-I for Eastern Canada.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88-DOHC||12-03-2001||04:45 PM||||Nice Shocks! Yes, you might want to do something about those.<P>About your front facia. Did you remove the honeycomb understructure, or is that exactly as it looked when you removed the nose?  If it is, then its missing the understructure. The metal bumper should be basicly completely flat across, other then the very ends. From the picture it looks ok, other then some rust (although it almost looks like part of each end has been cut off). Without the understructure, I would think the nose would end up sagging probably causing your problems.||||reg||1
Z||000005||jelly2m8||12-03-2001||04:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>So, I'm finally tearing into the car to see why the front end has funny gaps in the panels, and why the front turnsignals don't line up with the holes in the front fascia, and I find these...<P>  <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0OQAACP0TJDahq7QTV5Xtef4P8TrvCTNLL!*1jwlpUk2nQxKupHjnBXLcCQEkJPMBts7PpgLYVWrLcwgpzGi4yGCiCKPhTCkA/shock-close.jpg">  <P>My shocks in front both look like this...<BR>Is this a problem? (ugh!)<P>AND...<P>Can anyone tell me what's wrong with this fron end, under the fascia?<P>  <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0OwAAAPAUx2pfz5kYscXczhv29Kx7MhN54y8XnH0z1CBOaaZcaZckhtrhDy2hhrsnoye!ryc4a6Ctj**VvrOsWuQiBDTONA!*/front-exposed.jpg">  <P><BR>Any and all help is appreciated. Thanks.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Umm all i'm not seeing is the honeycomb support that goes behind the fascia.<P>Other than that, your coolant overflow jug has one of the ears busted, but that won't cause your problem... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>It's  not a real good quality shot ( thats fine, my pictures come out a lot worse),<BR>I can't get a good view of any frame damage, but nothing seems out or the ordinary. Just check for wrinkles, and crevices. If ya don't see anything bad, your probably alright.<P> Cant tell on the under side, is there a hanger on each side, under the headlight buckets?, also is there the steel support tray that connects the inner edge of the air dam to the lower rad support? Looks like the strap that supports the tray to the frame is still there.<P> My tip when you put it back together, is to align the front fascia up with the leading edges of the fenders first. then install the hood and worry about that afterward.<P> Theres a metal support along the front of the car, which the top edge of the fascia is bolted to. This can be adjusted to bring the upper edge of the fascia ahead, or back.<P> You can also get some up and down, side to side movement where the fascia bolts to this support.<P> If you get the top side edges of the fascia ( along the bottom edge of the fenders) tight to the bottom of the fenders, this will help the fit of the fascia around the leading edges of the fenders.<P> basically theres almost a kazillion different adjustments to be made to get everything lined up properly.<P> Start with the fascia and fenders, do the hood last. don't try and adjust them all at once, you'll go crazy! hehe.<P> let us know if you run into troubles aligning everything up, and where exactly you are having issues.<P>Oh ya the shocks,<P> Just the dust shield rusting off, not a safety issue (yet), but it's probably time for new ones.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||TRiAD||12-03-2001||04:57 PM||||Yeah, the honeycomb is in there, it wasn't mounted (at all) and I haven't tried putting it back on yet...I'm not even sure if it's the right one, I'll post a pic of it when I get there...<P><FONT COLOR="blue">Oh, how do I adjust the turn signal height?</FONT><P>Thanks!<P><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||12-03-2001||05:00 PM||||it may have the wrong rienforcement bar for the bumper you have.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88-DOHC||12-03-2001||05:06 PM||||Ah, if the honeycomb stuff wasn't even mounted, that would definitely cause excess stress on the facia (since its basicly just adding weight, instead of help supporting the whole mess).  Sounds like the plastic pressfit rivets either were removed and not replaced, or have broke. No big deal, just go to the hardware store and buy 4 bolts and washers, and just bolt it on.<P>BTW, did you have to remove 6 screws from below that attach to paper-like air guides into the radiator? It almost looks like those are missing as well (although you can get away with not having them).<P><BR>You can't adjust the actual signal height. If everything is as its suppose to be, you don't need to.<P><BR>One question, describe the things that are just to the inside and just below each blinker that stick out. If those are hard plastic and attached to the metal bumper, those are not suppose to be there.<p>[This message has been edited by 88-DOHC (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||TRiAD||12-03-2001||05:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88-DOHC:<BR><B>...BTW, did you have to remove 6 screws from below that attach to paper-like air guides into the radiator? It almost looks like those are missing as well (although you can get away with not having them).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, right under the radiator air inlet? They were there.<P>The problem I have now is, 2 of the screws holding it on (underneath, either side of the center hole) have snapped off, and I can't even drill them out to completely remove the fascia. Everything else is free...<P>What a mess...between this and the clutch pedal/etc, I'm tempted to sell this for parts and get one that works. <P>Anyone want a $2,150 parts car?<BR>...Neither did I. <BR>||||reg||9
Z||000010||California Kid||12-03-2001||05:12 PM||||Man...those shocks must be the original parts!!!<P>From your description, are you sure this car wasn't in some front end collision?? I think the only way you can dimensionally verify points is from a GM body service repair manual. You might post to see if anybody has one or access to one.||||reg||1
Z||000011||88-DOHC||12-03-2001||05:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B> Yeah, right under the radiator air inlet? They were there.<P>The problem I have now is, 2 of the screws holding it on (underneath, either side of the center hole) have snapped off, and I can't even drill them out to completely remove the fascia. Everything else is free...<P>What a mess...between this and the clutch pedal/etc, I'm tempted to sell this for parts and get one that works. <P>Anyone want a $2,150 parts car?<BR>...Neither did I. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>If I remember, all those screws go into is those threaded easy clip thingies in the facia. The paper-like air guides only have a metal reinforced holes and are not threaded. If you snapped a screw in those, just pry out the easy clip piece and replace it. It should keep you from completely removing the facia (if we are talking about the same fastening). If you snapped a screw in the metal hangers that each side plus the air dam attachs to, then thats something else. Even then you would be able to remove the hanger. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Pyric||12-03-2001||05:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0OQAACP0TJDahq7QTV5Xtef4P8TrvCTNLL!*1jwlpUk2nQxKupHjnBXLcCQEkJPMBts7PpgLYVWrLcwgpzGi4yGCiCKPhTCkA/shock-close.jpg">  <P>My shocks in front both look like this...<BR>Is this a problem? (ugh!)<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Ummm.... SOMEONE has been driving on the beach.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   <P>Are you the original owner of that car, because that looks like salt water induced rust to me.  May be from salting the roads in IL, but that's the kind of thing I'd expect to see on a car stored near the ocean or something.  <P>Bum deal.  Not going to be cheap to get rid of that rust.  It'll take a lot of work or a lot of money  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Don't give up though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||fropuff||12-03-2001||06:26 PM||||Might be the wrong front fascia support bar.  I don't remember mine looking like that, but mine's an '86 GT.  But then again, it's the same front fascia.  Hope you can fix it; looks damn nice!||||reg||1
Z||000014||DRH||12-03-2001||06:42 PM||||Yep, road salt can do that and worse. If the front end has that much rust, you might want to check the back closely. In particular, check the bottom of the trunk on the sides where it's not deep and the body underneath the plastic inner fender wells.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Jefrysuko||12-03-2001||06:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pyric:<BR><B>Not going to be cheap to get rid of that rust.  It'll take a lot of work or a lot of money   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's only a shock and from what I remember Triad is planning on autocrossing the car and would want to replace them anyway.<P>Triad,  From what I can see here everything looks good for the most part.  Get some straight edges and check to see if flat across the front and at the same angle with the ground on both sides.  Seeing that nothing is obviously bent tells me that their most likely isn't anything to worry about as far as the frame being straight.  From my experience the turn signals do sit slightly higher than the holes in the fascia but that might be because I am used to seeing ones that sag.  I can't remember exactly but I think you might be able to elongate the holes in the brackets and move them down a little or make your own brackets and put them where you want.<P>I know that is is frustrating finding things wrong with your car but it is one hell of a nice looking one and I personally won't let you downgrade it to a parts car.  I am sure that you really don't want to either.  Keep in there and you will get everything worked out and in the long run you will look back and laugh about buying a car with holes in the shocks.||||reg||1
Z||000016||avengador1||12-03-2001||07:16 PM||||It looks to me like the passenger front side had a little tap and got crinkled a bit, but otherwise it looks relatively straight. The shocks definitly need to be replaced. It's going to take time and money, but with a little perseverance you should be able to get your car to like new condition. Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||TRiAD||12-04-2001||01:14 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B>...Keep in there and you will get everything worked out and in the long run you will look back and laugh about buying a car with holes in the shocks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>God, I hope so...<P>I've been laid off since July and have our 3rd child on the way...I don't know if I have the resources to get this going again...<P>Man, I'm frustrated...<P>Sorry...<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||88-DOHC||12-04-2001||01:23 AM||||Something I have been meaning to ask, exactly how do lights not align up?  Are they too low/high? To the right/left of the fascia openings? <P>It might be more clear if you had some additional pictures of the front area in close up form.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||TRiAD||12-04-2001||01:27 AM||||The lights are too high for the holes...barely visible...it's apart now, so I have no good pics of this...sorry.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Cheever3000||12-04-2001||02:11 AM||||Maybe this isn't a great comparison, but it might help a little...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Casual4.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||88-DOHC||12-04-2001||02:22 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>The lights are too high for the holes...barely visible...it's apart now, so I have no good pics of this...sorry.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, I meant more pictures of it apart, much like you posted earlier, just closer.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Particularly, you never answered my question about those things that seem to be sticking out that are just to the inside and below the lights. I can't really tell from the pics whether they are some sort of hard plastic things, or just the paper-like air guides.<P>Since the lights are too high for the holes, that means the fascia is sagging. So either something is forcing it to sag, or its just because the honeycomb material wasn't attached to the bumper (thus helping support the nose).<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88-DOHC (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||Eriamjh||12-04-2001||07:22 AM||||The part of the shock that is rusted is only the metal sleeve or dust shield, not the actual shock itself.  So you replace it and move on.<P>As for your front end, I can't give any advice.||||reg||1
Z||000023||BOILERMAKER||12-04-2001||07:41 AM||||It does look like you might have possibly gotten tapped on the right side, but other than a slight tap, it doesn't look bad at all.  I tackle the mounting of the honeycomb first, then see if the gaps can be re-aligned to your liking.<P>Front shocks are a piece of cake.  The only thing I would worry about is the bottom bolt as it is probably seized by the rust to the shock, but that can be solved with penetrant in most cases.||||reg||1
Z||000024||TRiAD||12-04-2001||10:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88-DOHC:<BR><B>...you never answered my question about those things that seem to be sticking out that are just to the inside and below the lights. I can't really tell from the pics whether they are some sort of hard plastic things, or just the paper-like air guides...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They're hard plastic, just like the crush material. <P>I'll try to get a closer pic for you of the front...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||dcaprio||12-04-2001||01:02 PM||||It's hard to tell from the pics but it looks like you don't have the brackets that attatch the blinkers to the bumper. The look like they are screwed right to the bumper like non areo cars.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Jefrysuko||12-04-2001||01:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dcaprio:<BR><B>It's hard to tell from the pics but it looks like you don't have the brackets that attatch the blinkers to the bumper. The look like they are screwed right to the bumper like non areo cars.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>His blinkers are attached the same way in which they are attached on my 86 SE and 88 GT.  Would you care to elaborate on what you think he is missing?||||reg||1
