Z||000030||TRiAD||12-05-2001||10:52 AM||||OK, here's a few pics...<P>Crush Material (not in intended location)<P>     <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0QAB0cBwWd9XTQIW8I7Q*1xVhLYpraldtvazYsMWgArtIzKtNT18iWMF7lvYlaP2yRpeyeOLVefU0u4PLRvxNguzhM7rDV*4zAAAAAAAAAAA/crush-material.jpg">     <P>And a close-up of the signal area<BR> <A HREF="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0SAAAAM4Y1Y5fz5kYscXczg4l35LK6jcVvcN7ruMZPLkNbEjhfEQvH*EJNk6dScgb4tE7AqxnmiVu1JNB5YWt0zd9yWoV9CsW8Zp8SZzrqGFpAG4ALgBwAA/front-signal-exposed" TARGET=_blank>http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0SAAAAM4Y1Y5fz5kYscXczg4l35LK6jcVvcN7ruMZPLkNbEjhfEQvH*EJNk6dScgb4tE7AqxnmiVu1JNB5YWt0zd9yWoV9CsW8Zp8SZzrqGFpAG4ALgBwAA/front-signal-exposed</A>  .jpg <P>...You're going to have to find this last one...PFF keeps putting a space after "exposed" that isn't there...I've edited like 20 times and can't get it linked...I've got to get back to the car...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><BR>Hope these help...Thanks!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||88-DOHC||12-05-2001||05:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>OK, here's a few pics...<P>Crush Material (not in intended location)<BR>.<BR>.<BR>...You're going to have to find this last one...PFF keeps putting a space after "exposed" that isn't there...I've edited like 20 times and can't get it linked...I've got to get back to the car...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><BR>Hope these help...Thanks!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmm... Oddly I have never seen those extra hard plastic crush material that is just to the inside of each turn light. I have seen something kind of similar directly under each light (but turned horizontal) that help support the part of the light hole in the fascia.  Anyway, I would say, attach your backing structure (honeycomb stuff). Just go to the hardware store and get some bolts and washers(1 flat washer, and 1 lock washer per bolt) that fit the 4 holes that are along the exact middle (1 at each end, 2 in the middle area) of the metal bumper, and bolt it in. The upper support bar looks ok from the picture, but just make sure it doesn't have any obvious bends or twists in it (its also easier if you leave that attached to the fascia). Once you have the honeycomb structure reattached, test fit the nose, and see how it fits. If it doesn't fit, look to see if there is anything that is keeping it from being where its suppose to be.  It might be that those hard plastic peices that I havn't ever seen (or noticed) on all the cars I have taken noses off, is causing your problems. Most likely its just because the honeycomb backing material wasn't mounted and wasn't help supporting the nose.||||reg||1
Z||000032||jelly2m8||12-05-2001||06:20 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B> His blinkers are attached the same way in which they are attached on my 86 SE and 88 GT.  Would you care to elaborate on what you think he is missing?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>dcaprio is talking about the brackets that bolt to the bumper, then the signal lights screw to this. These brackets lower, and bring the signal lights ahead further, makes a huge improvement.<P> I have only seen these twice on a fiero, both were on a 87 and a 88 coupe.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000033||88-DOHC||12-05-2001||06:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B>  dcaprio is talking about the brackets that bolt to the bumper, then the signal lights screw to this. These brackets lower, and bring the signal lights ahead further, makes a huge improvement.<P> I have only seen these twice on a fiero, both were on a 87 and a 88 coupe.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The brackets are there. On later models I believe those brackets were bolted to the metal bumper and not spot welded on as is case on Triad's car. I don't recall the brackets ever lowering the lights, just bringing them out away from the metal bumper and giving them a place to be mounted.<BR>||||reg||1
